Cancelling the quit confirmation on macOS leaves `state.quitRequested` set to `true`, and `closeAllDevTunnels()` has already run. From then on the app believes it is quitting while it keeps running.

## Problem

`app.on('before-quit')` sets the flag and tears down tunnels before it knows whether the user will confirm:

```js
// packages/electron/main.mjs:5714
app.on('before-quit', (event) => {
  state.quitRequested = true;
  closeAllDevTunnels();

  if (state.installingUpdate) {
    return;
  }

  if (process.platform === 'darwin' && !state.quitConfirmed) {
    event.preventDefault();
    void requestQuitWithConfirmation();
    return;
  }
  ...
});
```

The macOS confirmation dialog is shown only when a tunnel is active or a scheduled task is enabled or running (`shouldRequireQuitConfirmation`, main.mjs:394-397). The Cancel branch resets just one flag:

```js
// packages/electron/main.mjs:582
state.quitConfirmationPending = false;
if (result.response === 0) {
  performConfirmedQuit();
}
```

On Cancel (`response === 1`), `state.quitRequested` stays `true`. Nothing else resets it outside of two update-install paths: `rollbackQuitState` at main.mjs:3402-3406 already does `state.quitRequested = false` when an abandoned quit has to be undone, and the Linux installer handoff sets it at main.mjs:3460.

## What the stale flag breaks

The window close handler gates every hide path on `!state.quitRequested`:

```js
// packages/electron/main.mjs:2496
browserWindow.on('close', (event) => {
  if (!state.quitRequested && shouldHideMainWindowToTray(browserWindow)) { ... hide ... }
  if (!state.quitRequested && shouldHideMainWindowToQuake(browserWindow)) { ... hide ... }
  if (process.platform === 'darwin' && !state.quitRequested && !browserWindow.__ocContextWindowOwnerId) { ... hide ... }
```

So after a cancelled quit, closing the window destroys it instead of hiding it to the tray or quake window, and the macOS hide-on-last-window behavior is skipped too. `window-all-closed` then returns early only when the flag is `false` (main.mjs:5695-5697), so with `true` the app stays alive with no windows.

The tunnels are also gone: `closeAllDevTunnels()` closes the relay tunnels, space preview ports, and the dev tunnel client (main.mjs:3802-3813) before any confirmation. A user who cancels to keep working has already lost those.

## Impact

On macOS, Cmd+Q followed by Cancel turns the next Cmd+W into a window destroy. The main window disappears, the app keeps running, and someone who relies on hide-to-tray or the quake window loses it until they reopen from the tray. Tunnels that were active when the dialog appeared are already closed even though the quit was cancelled. A second Cmd+Q still reaches the dialog again, so this does not strand quitting; it strands the close behavior.

## Acceptance criteria

- Cancelling the quit confirmation restores `state.quitRequested` to `false`, and the next window close hides to tray, hides the quake window, or hides on macOS exactly as it did before Cmd+Q.
- `closeAllDevTunnels()` does not run when the quit is cancelled, or the cancel path restores what it closed. Moving the teardown into `performConfirmedQuit` is one option.
- A test pins the lifecycle: `before-quit` followed by a cancelled dialog leaves `quitRequested` false and the close handler still calls the hide path; a confirmed dialog still exits.
- A quit that follows a cancelled quit still exits.

